 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-that-breaks-its-own-render-is-told-and-goes-on"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-02 by the context lab's curation pilot (a scripted replay of task 618a903ed3 on a reverse-patched tree; repro in /private/tmp/seon-l2r/618a903ed3/repro-no-end/README.md): turn 3 made three calls; edit! of seon.ctx/members-map was refused (its old text ended in a newline the recorded form lacks), the next call's edit! of seon.ctx/join applied anyway (join now calls (members-map pairs flag)), and the agent's next context render threw; its following turn threw the same way and the run ended :no-end, \"two turns in a row threw\" (seon.turn/work!), the error never reaching the model. Two causes, each general: (1) a call whose answer is an error value (a refused edit!, any :seon.error map) does not stop the reply's later calls, though `seon.turn/act!` stops them after a throw, so a later call builds on a change that did not happen; (2) an agent that breaks code its own turn loop runs (seon.ctx, the render, seon.ctx: Seon improves itself) can no longer be sent anything, since the request is drawn by that code, so 'a turn that threw is told' cannot tell it. Correct: (1) a call answering an error value stops the reply's later calls as a throw does (seon.repl/unrun's not-run, the same one result); (2) a turn whose request cannot be drawn sends the previous request as it was sent (the prefix the cache already holds, seon.ctx.wire/request) with the error as one message printed by the one printer, so the agent reads why and fixes its branch; the run ends only by the existing caps (max-turns), never because the agent's own code broke its page. Owner's ruling 2026-10-28 (never crash, never hang: errors go to whoever can fix them). Design (2) to be shown to the owner before it is built."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/an-error-answer-stops-the-replys-later-calls seon.turn-test/a-turn-whose-page-cannot-be-drawn-sends-its-error}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-turn-is-a-turn-with-only-the-merge-left"
  :seon.task/plan "Reproduction (2026-11-10, Context, a test root from jolt/request-launch, store .worktrees/request-launch/runs/store): request/add-a-function-demo-536871029's agent never named a proof; two siblings merged demo.math/triple first, so its status held an unmerged entry; after merging main, root closed it held 'ended merge-failed (3 turns without green > :seon.config.merge/max-merge-turns 2): no-proofs'. Cause: seon.turn counts a merge turn on every turn of a merging agent while any unmerged entry is open, whatever else the done rule says, so the task's own work counted as merging. Plan: a turn counts as a merge turn only when the done rule's reasons, minus :unmerged, are empty (the unmerged entries are all that stands between the task and green); any other open reason is the task's own work, counted against :seon.config.agent/max-turns only, and the agent is told its first reason. It deletes the whole?/merge-task? case in the count: a merge task's only reason is its unmerged entries. Proof, red first: a task with :no-proofs and an unmerged entry runs past max-merge-turns turns and is not closed merge-failed; once its proof is named and green, its next turns with only :unmerged left count, and past the limit it is closed."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
